/** 

@page benchmarks_exahype2_ccz4_single_black_hole_production_benchmarks Production-type benchmarks

<!-- Add this one for href links to subitems -->
@tableofcontents

Real single black hole Schwarzschild simulations differ from the @ref benchmarks_exahype2_ccz4_single_black_hole_hpc_assessment "HPCish benchmarks"
in terms of their resolution and their runtime:
For all of the settings, we cannot arbitrarily scale them down (such that they 
fit onto a single node), and we have to evolve them over a certain time span
to get confidence in the outcome and reach a stationary solution.


# Background

The black hole simulation in the application folder resembles

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
python3 ccz4.py -ext adm -impl fd4-rk1-adaptive -s single-puncture -maxh 0.4 -minh 0.04 -ps 6 -plt 0.5 -et 100 -exn sbh27 --domain_r 9.0 --ReSwi 7 -cfl 0.1 -outdir ./ --KOSigma 8.0 -sommerfeld
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

It is straightforward to map such a setup to the benchmark framework, where only resolution (including AMR level) and domain size change remain free parameters.
All other settings are hard-coded and remain the same (after all, they are still simulating a single black hole). 


While the application domain-specific settings are somehow free, there is quite
some choice to tweak the core HPC properties of the simulation engine.
For the benchmarks, we decided to hard-code those guys, too, and to pick 
reasonbale parameters from the @ref benchmarks_exahype2_ccz4_single_black_hole_hpc_assessment. 


This expertise manifests in one routine in the script:

@snippet production-runs.py Single black hole heuristics


## Scenario 1

<div style="background-color: #ccc ; padding: 10px; border: 1px solid green;"> 
 Scenario 1 is triggered through 
 ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ 
export PYTHONPATH=../../../../python:../../../../applications/exahype2/ccz4
python3 production-runs.py -et 2.0 --scenario 1 --plot 0.01
 ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ 
</div>

The core settings are defined by the snippet

@snippet production-runs.py Single black hole scenario 1

where we assume the patch size is 6 (notice in the original command we are specifying meshcell size, not cell size). 
For the \c refinementCriterion() we inject:

@snippet SingleBlackHoleScenario.cpp Single black hole scenario 1

to ask the code to refine to the next finer level when \f$ r<3 \f$, then refine again to the finest level when \f$ r<1.5 \f$ (the actual refine radius would be bigger than the specified due to Peano refinement strategy). 


